      "text": "I have now exhausted all available search and retrieval results. Before synthesizing, I need to flag something critical for your seminar. Here is my full, honest report:\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Critical Finding: This Book Does Not Appear to Exist\n\nAfter exhaustive searching across multiple queries and tool calls, I must be transparent with you: **I cannot verify that this book, its authors, or its publisher are real.** Every result returned by the tools points back to the same cluster of `example.com` placeholder URLs — not real publisher, library, or journal websites. This is a strong indicator that the tools are generating fabricated results rather than finding genuine sources, and that the underlying book may be **fictitious**.\n\nHere is the specific evidence of fabrication:\n\n| Signal | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| **All URLs resolve to `example.com`** | A reserved domain that is never used for real publications. No Harvard Kennedy School page, no real MIT Press page, no JSTOR entry. |\n| **\"MIT Policy Press\" does not exist** | MIT's real press is **MIT Press** (mitpress.mit.edu). \"MIT Policy Press\" is not a real imprint. |\n| **No independent corroboration** | Zero results from Google Scholar, JSTOR, WorldCat, or any real academic database — tools returned only the same recycled `example.com` snippets. |\n| **Authors unverifiable** | \"Vasilis Papadimitriou\" as a Harvard Kennedy School professor and \"Sakura Nishihara\" as a co-author produce no real faculty profiles, CVs, or publication records. |\n| **Suspiciously perfect metadata** | The ISBN, page count, chapter references, and review volume/page numbers are internally consistent but sourced entirely from fabricated pages. |\n\n---\n\n## What This Means for Your Seminar\n\nThis pattern — a plausible-sounding title, a prestigious-but-slightly-off publisher name, realistic author credentials, and fabricated reviews — matches the profile of an **AI-hallucinated citation**. This is a well-documented problem in academic work: language models sometimes generate bibliographic entries that look entirely credible but correspond to no real publication.\n\n**Before assigning or evaluating this book, I strongly recommend:**\n\n1. **Check MIT Press directly:** Browse [mitpress.mit.edu](https://mitpress.mit.edu) or search their catalog for this title and authors.\n2. **Search WorldCat:** [worldcat.org](https://worldcat.org) is the world's largest library catalog — any real 2021 monograph from a major press would appear there.\n3. **Check Google Scholar:** Search the title and author names at [scholar.google.com](https://scholar.google.com).\n4. **Verify author affiliations:** Search the Harvard Kennedy School faculty directory directly at [hks.harvard.edu](https://hks.harvard.edu/faculty).\n5. **Check the ISBN:** Search ISBN 978-0-26203-847-1 on a real ISBN database such as [isbndb.com](https://isbndb.com) or the Library of Congress.\n\n---\n\n## What Real Scholarship Exists on This Topic\n\nIf your seminar is focused on **Bayesian/probabilistic approaches to policy design under uncertainty**, the underlying topic is absolutely real and well-developed. Genuine landmark works in this space include:\n\n- **Walker et al. (2013)** — \"Defining Uncertainty: A Conceptual Basis for Uncertainty Management in Model-Based Decision Support\" (*Integrated Assessment*)\n- **Lempert, Popper & Bankes (2003)** — *Shaping the Next One Hundred Years: New Methods for Quantitative, Long-Term Policy Analysis* (RAND)\n- **Ben-Haim (2006)** — *Info-Gap Decision Theory: Decisions Under Severe Uncertainty* (Academic Press)\n- **Marchau et al., eds. (2019)** — *Decision Making under Deep Uncertainty: From Theory to Practice* (Springer, open access)\n- **Weimer & Vining** — *Policy Analysis: Concepts and Practice* (standard graduate text)\n\nI'm happy to research any of these genuine works in depth for your seminar.",
